<StoryLayout story={story}>
  <div class="container">

<section class="section" id="allegation">
  <h2><span class="num">01 — The Allegation</span>"Without Any Discretionary Human Thought"</h2>
  <p>On August 12, 2026, U.S. District Judge Gloria Navarro assumed — for the sake of argument — that a Nevada state family court judge had relied wholly on artificial intelligence to issue a ruling, without any discretionary human thought. Then she held that it made no legal difference.</p>
  <p>The case was <em>Phillips v. Parlade</em>, No. 2:25-cv-01464-GMN-NJK, in the U.S. District Court for the District of Nevada. T. Matthew Phillips had sued Judge Mari D. Parlade of the Eighth Judicial District Court, Family Division, Clark County — the judge assigned to his state custody case since Parlade took the bench in January 2023. The underlying dispute was a family law matter, <em>Phillips v. Phillips</em>, in which the Nevada Supreme Court had ruled on custody in April 2022. What Phillips challenged in federal court was a subsequent ruling by Parlade.</p>
  <p>The case reached Judge Navarro on review of a Report and Recommendation by Magistrate Judge Nancy J. Koppe, who had recommended dismissal with prejudice. Phillips objected. The court reviewed those objections de novo (from scratch, without deference to the magistrate's recommendation). The objection that mattered was this: judicial immunity should not apply because, Phillips argued, the state judge had not performed a judicial act at all.</p>
  <p>His language was specific. As the opinion recounts, Phillips argued "that judicial immunity does not apply in this matter because Defendant unlawfully delegated her official decision-making duties when she relied wholly on artificial intelligence to issue a judicial ruling, without any discretionary human thought, such that her actions cannot be considered a 'judicial act.'" He pressed the point further: "because Defendant delegated 100% of her decision-making duties, the rulings were in clear absence of all jurisdiction."</p>

