  <p>No prior court appears to have addressed the question of whether a judge's alleged use of AI should overcome judicial immunity. No commentator who covered the opinion — including legal scholar Eugene Volokh, who published the first analysis on August 17, 2026 — identified a precedent. The argument was novel. The answer was not.</p>
</section>

<div class="phantom-wrap">
  <canvas class="phillips-parlade-judicial-immunity-canvas" aria-hidden="true"></canvas>
</div>
<p class="canvas-note"><strong>The Function</strong> — Rings drift in a dark field; some hold a faint inner light, some are hollow. A slow scan pulse sweeps outward and illuminates every outer edge identically, then fades. The test reads surfaces. What is inside each ring never changes, and the scan never reaches it.</p>

<section class="section" id="doctrine">
  <h2><span class="num">02 — The Doctrine</span>A 154-Year-Old Answer</h2>
  <p>The court's answer came from a doctrine older than the telephone.</p>
  <p>Judges enjoy absolute immunity from civil liability for acts performed in their judicial capacity. The standard, as the Navarro opinion restated it from <em>Meek v. County of Riverside</em> (9th Cir. 1999), is that judges are immune "even if their action was in error, done maliciously, or in excess of their authority." This language is not a novel pronouncement. It traces through <em>Stump v. Sparkman</em> (1978) to <em>Bradley v. Fisher</em> (1872) — 154 years of continuous doctrine. The court was applying settled law.</p>
  <p>Two exceptions exist, both established in <em>Mireles v. Waco</em> (1991). Judicial immunity does not apply when the challenged conduct involves "a clear absence of all jurisdiction" or when the conduct is "not judicial in nature." Phillips tried to reach the first exception — his argument was that total delegation of decision-making constituted an absence of jurisdiction. To evaluate the second, the court applied a four-factor test from <em>Meek</em>, which asks whether challenged conduct is "judicial in nature."</p>
  <p>The four factors examine: (1) whether the act is a normal judicial function, (2) whether the events occurred in the judge's chambers, (3) whether the controversy centered on a case pending before the judge, and (4) whether the events arose out of a confrontation with the judge in an official capacity.</p>

  <div class="ppji-meek-wrap">
    <div class="ppji-meek-title">Meek v. Cnty. of Riverside — Four-Factor Test for Judicial Acts</div>
    <div class="ppji-meek-row">
