      <span class="ppji-meek-num">4</span>
      <span class="ppji-meek-question">Did events arise from a confrontation in the judge's official capacity?</span>
      <span class="ppji-meek-examines">The Parties</span>
      <span class="ppji-meek-status ppji-meek-check" role="img" aria-label="Satisfied">&#10003;</span>
    </div>
    <div class="ppji-meek-row ppji-meek-ghost">
      <span class="ppji-meek-num">&mdash;</span>
      <span class="ppji-meek-question">Was human judgment exercised in reaching the decision?</span>
      <span class="ppji-meek-examines">The Process</span>
      <span class="ppji-meek-status">Not Asked</span>
    </div>
    <div class="ppji-meek-footer">The test is complete. The question the plaintiff cared about was never on it.</div>
  </div>

  <p>None of the four factors asks <em>how</em> the decision was reached. The test is functional: it examines what act was performed, never what process produced it. It asks about the nature of the output, not the nature of the reasoning.</p>
  <p>The court's application was terse. "Issuing a judicial ruling is clearly a normal judicial function and the controversy at issue centered around Plaintiff's state court case pending before Defendant." On the jurisdiction exception, the court found that "Plaintiff provides no case law or authority to support a finding that the challenged conduct was accompanied by a clear absence of all jurisdiction." Immunity applied.</p>
  <p>The court expressly did not determine whether Judge Parlade actually used AI. The opinion states only that "Plaintiff alleges that Defendant issued a judicial decision in his state court case by relying wholly on artificial intelligence." The word "alleges" does all the work. The allegation was assumed. The functional test was applied. The result was the same regardless.</p>
  <p>The complaint was dismissed with prejudice — meaning it cannot be refiled.</p>
</section>

<section class="section" id="disconnect">
  <h2><span class="num">03 — The Disconnect</span>Justified by What It Cannot Verify</h2>
  <p>Absolute judicial immunity exists to protect the exercise of independent judgment. The doctrine's justification is specific: judges must be free to decide cases without fear that a disappointed litigant will retaliate through civil suit. If every ruling could expose a judge to personal liability, the argument goes, judges would decide not according to the law but according to the risk of being sued. The protection of discretion is not a side effect. It is the stated purpose.</p>
  <p>Phillips located the exact seam in this justification. If judicial immunity protects the exercise of discretion, what does the doctrine protect when the discretion is alleged to be absent? If the entire justification rests on safeguarding human judgment, and the claim is that no human judgment occurred, then the shield and its rationale come apart. It is a structurally coherent argument.</p>
  <p>The court's answer reveals a structural feature of the doctrine, not a flaw introduced by AI. The four-factor <em>Meek</em> test has never asked whether judgment was exercised. It has never examined the mental process behind a judicial decision. This is not an oversight. It is 154 years of deliberate design. Any test that inquired into <em>how</em> a judge reached a decision would itself become a vehicle for harassing judges through litigation. A losing party could allege that the judge did not think carefully enough, did not read the briefs, did not consider the evidence with sufficient attention. The doctrine forecloses that entire category of inquiry by refusing to ask the question at all.</p>
  <p>The consequence is precise. The same design that prevents frivolous interrogation of judicial reasoning also prevents any inquiry into whether reasoning occurred. The doctrine protects the institution by making the judge's internal process unreachable — and that unreachability does not vary based on what the internal process was.</p>
</section>

<section class="section" id="plaintiff">
  <h2><span class="num">04 — The Plaintiff</span>Almost Certainly Wrong</h2>
  <p>The honest assessment of Phillips's AI allegation is that it was almost certainly wrong.</p>
  <p>According to Above the Law, citing Magistrate Judge Koppe's Report and Recommendation, Phillips's evidence that Judge Parlade used AI consisted of the following: the judge read from a prepared script during proceedings, stumbled over words, and at one point said "appearance of any impartiality" when the phrase she appeared to be reading was "appearance of impropriety." Above the Law's own characterization of this evidence was direct: it is "weak." Judges routinely read from prepared remarks. Stumbling over a word is not evidence of anything other than speaking. Misreading "impropriety" as "impartiality" is a slip, not a signature of machine-generated text.</p>
  <p>According to Above the Law's reporting on the Koppe R&amp;R, this was reportedly at least Phillips's tenth federal challenge to his state court custody case, with zero successful outcomes. The underlying dispute — <em>Phillips v. Phillips</em> — was decided by the Nevada Supreme Court in April 2022, awarding sole legal and primary physical custody to the respondent based on a domestic violence presumption. Phillips has been litigating its aftermath since. The Koppe R&amp;R was not independently accessed for this story; all characterization of Phillips's litigation history derives from Above the Law's reporting on that document.</p>
  <p>Phillips was almost certainly wrong about the AI allegation. But "almost certainly" is the strongest conclusion the legal system can produce on this question, and it would remain the strongest conclusion even if he were right. The court did not investigate the allegation because the doctrine did not require it. No other body investigated it either. No mechanism exists that could move the system's answer from "probably not" to "certainly not" — or, if the facts were different, from "possibly" to "confirmed."</p>
  <p>That is the structural point the case makes regardless of the plaintiff's credibility. Phillips identified a question the system cannot answer. The system responded — correctly, under existing doctrine — that it does not need to answer it. Both of those things are true at the same time.</p>
</section>

<section class="section" id="topology">
  <h2><span class="num">05 — The Topology</span>What Nothing Can Detect</h2>
  <p>If the doctrine will not ask whether a judge used AI, the question is whether anything else in the system can. Walk each accountability channel in turn.</p>
  <p><strong>Appellate review</strong> examines whether a lower court applied the correct legal standard, evaluated the evidence properly, and reached a supportable conclusion. It reviews the output. A ruling that applies the right standard to the facts, reasons through them competently, and reaches a defensible conclusion will survive appellate review regardless of whether a human mind or a language model drafted the analysis. Appeal can detect legal error. It cannot detect the absence of human engagement with the merits.</p>
  <p><strong>State judicial discipline</strong>, in Nevada, operates through the Commission on Judicial Discipline, which investigates violations of the Revised Nevada Code of Judicial Conduct and failure to perform duties of office. The commission has no AI-specific rules. Nevada has no AI disclosure requirement at any level of its state court system. The Nevada Supreme Court has not issued an administrative order on the subject. A complaint alleging undisclosed AI use would need to identify a rule that was violated. No such rule exists.</p>
  <p><strong>Federal interim guidance</strong>, issued Judiciary-wide on July 31, 2025, by the Judicial Conference's AI Task Force, "cautions against delegating core judicial functions to AI, including decision-making or case adjudication." It "recommends that users exercise extreme caution especially if using AI to aid in addressing novel legal questions." On disclosure, it "recommends that AI users consider whether the use of AI should be disclosed." It "reminds judges and Judiciary users and those who approve the use of AI that they are accountable for all work performed with the assistance of AI." Every operative verb — cautions, recommends, reminds — is advisory. The guidance does not prohibit. It does not mandate. It does not create an enforceable disclosure obligation.</p>
  <p><strong>National tracking</strong> does not exist. In an October 21, 2025, letter to Senate Judiciary Chairman Chuck Grassley, Robert J. Conrad Jr., Director of the Administrative Office of U.S. Courts, wrote that the judiciary is "aware anecdotally of incidents in which judges have taken official action ... relating to the integrity of court filings in which the use of AI tools was in question, although we currently do not systematically track such activity at the national level." The incidents Conrad referenced were litigant-side — judges sanctioning parties for AI-generated errors in filings, not judges' own use of AI. The federal judiciary does not systematically track AI-related incidents at any level. There is no infrastructure that would detect judicial-side AI use either.</p>

