    <div class="ppji-channels-summary">Detection surface for undisclosed use that produces competent output: none.</div>
  </div>

  <p>A Northwestern University / Sedona Conference survey of federal judges, published March 30, 2026, found that more than 60 percent of responding judges already use at least one AI tool in their judicial work. The survey reached 112 of 502 randomly selected federal judges — a 22.3 percent response rate, which limits generalizability. One further figure from the same survey is worth isolating. Of the judges who responded, 45.5 percent said AI training was not provided by their court administration.</p>
  <p>The ABA Task Force on Law and Artificial Intelligence published its Year 2 Report on December 15, 2025, which included guidelines for judicial officers. Its core principle: "AI may assist judges, but it can never replace judicial judgment; judges remain solely responsible for decisions issued in their names." These are professional guidelines. They are not enforceable rules with a detection mechanism.</p>
  <p>The cases where AI use in federal court orders has been detected are instructive. U.S. District Judge Julien Xavier Neals of New Jersey issued an opinion in June 2025 containing fabricated case citations traced to a law school intern's use of ChatGPT. U.S. District Judge Henry T. Wingate of Mississippi had a law clerk use Perplexity as a drafting assistant, producing errors. Both orders were retracted. Both were detectable because the outputs contained hallucinated citations — errors that were checkable against the record. Undisclosed use that produces plausible output has no equivalent detection surface.</p>
  <p>No mechanism in the current system is designed to detect undisclosed AI use by a judge. Every channel assumes either that errors will be visible in the output, that someone will file a complaint identifying a violated rule, or that the user will volunteer the information. None can reach undisclosed use that produces facially competent work.</p>
</section>

<section class="section" id="counterpoint">
  <h2><span class="num">06 — The Counterpoint</span>The Only Source of Visibility</h2>
  <p>Six days before Judge Navarro filed the Phillips opinion, on August 6, 2026, Connecticut Superior Court Judge Walter M. Spader Jr. did something no doctrine required. He disclosed his use of AI tools in a published decision.</p>
  <p>The case was a sanctions motion. Matthew Elliott, a self-represented plaintiff in <em>Elliott v. New York Bariatric Group</em> (Case No. AAN-CV-25-6066141-S, Connecticut Superior Court), had embedded hidden prompt-injection text — white three-point font on a white background — in court filings. Spader sanctioned him. In the same decision, according to Volokh's reporting on the fourteen-page opinion, Spader disclosed that he had used Google's Gemini to produce a working English translation of a Brazilian court decision and used Westlaw's Precision AI review features to check authorities and legal principles. He noted that Microsoft Word and Google Docs spelling and grammar-checking features are themselves AI-powered. He disclosed those too.</p>
  <p>Spader drew a line. "Judgment can never be delegated to a machine in any profession, but most importantly in the legal field," he wrote, according to the decision as reported by Volokh. "The judgment, reasoning and the decision remain the undersigned's."</p>
  <p>Spader's disclosure was not a legal obligation. No rule in Connecticut or any other state requires it. It was a choice. Voluntary disclosure by individual judges is currently the only source of visibility the system has into judicial AI use. The accountability architecture that the Phillips opinion implicitly relies on — the notion that remedy lies in appeal and discipline — cannot produce this visibility. Only the judge can. Spader's disclosure and Navarro's ruling arrived within six days of each other. One judge chose to show his work. The other's ruling confirmed that no one can be made to.</p>
  <p>The system's capacity to know whether a judge used AI depends entirely on the judge choosing to say so. <em>Phillips v. Parlade</em> confirms that the doctrine will not ask. The interim guidance recommends but does not require. The discipline commissions have no applicable rule. The appellate courts review the output, not the process. The only window is the one the judge opens voluntarily. And when the judge does not, the system's answer, at every level, is the same one Judge Navarro gave: we do not reach that question.</p>
</section>

